Summary: Run mvn clean install to rebuild the project with updated Jackson dependencies

Reasoning:
Okay, so I've just made a couple of key updates to our project's dependencies. Specifically, I've gone into the `dependencyManagement` section of our root `pom.xml` and introduced the 
`jackson-core` and `jackson-databind` libraries, making sure to pin them to their latest, presumably most secure and feature-rich, versions. This is a standard practice for us when 
managing our core libraries, ensuring we're leveraging the most current stable releases.

My next step, naturally, is to integrate these changes fully into the project build. I'll be kicking off a `mvn clean install`. This command is crucial because it cleans out any previous 
build artifacts and then recompiles everything from scratch, including incorporating these new Jackson versions. It's our way of ensuring a clean slate and verifying that the project 
still compiles and runs as expected with the updated dependencies.

Once that build is complete and successful, my attention will immediately shift to security. I'll be running `osv-scanner`. This is our go-to tool for proactively identifying any known 
vulnerabilities within our dependencies. The idea is that by updating core libraries like Jackson, we're not only getting the latest features but also patching any security holes that 
might have existed in older versions. `osv-scanner` will then be the final arbiter, confirming whether this update has indeed eliminated any previously identified risks, or if any new 
ones have cropped up (which is less likely with an update but always a possibility we need to check). This two-pronged approach – updating and then scanning – is fundamental to 
maintaining our project's integrity and security posture.

